## 4. Comparison of the Three Glacier Storage Classes

| Feature | Glacier Instant Retrieval | Glacier Flexible Retrieval | Glacier Deep Archive |
|---|---|---|---|
| Main purpose | Rare access with immediate retrieval | Flexible archival retrieval | Very long-term archival |
| Direct access | Yes | Restore required | Restore required |
| Typical retrieval | Milliseconds | Minutes to hours | Hours |
| Minimum storage duration | 90 days | 90 days | 180 days |
| Expedited retrieval | Not applicable | Available | Not available |
| Standard retrieval | Not applicable | Typically 3–5 hours | Typically within 12 hours |
| Bulk retrieval | Not applicable | Typically 5–12 hours | Typically within 48 hours |
| Relative storage cost | Low | Lower than Instant Retrieval in many comparisons | Generally the lowest |
| Best use case | Archived data needed immediately | Occasional backup restoration | Rarely accessed long-term archives |

*Retrieval times are typical, not guaranteed completion times. Actual performance and charges depend on the retrieval option, object size, workload, Region, and current AWS pricing.*

## 5. How S3 Glacier Works

A typical archival workflow looks like this:

```text
          Application / User
                  |
                  v
           Amazon S3 Bucket
                  |
                  v
           S3 Lifecycle Rule
                  |
                  v
       Eligible objects age over time
                  |
                  v
        S3 Glacier Storage Class
                  |
         +--------+---------+
         |                  |
         v                  v
  Instant Retrieval   Flexible / Deep Archive
         |                  |
         v                  v
    Direct access      Restore request
                            |
                            v
                     Retrieval completes
                            |
                            v
                    Temporary copy available
```

### Step-by-Step Explanation

1. An application uploads objects to an S3 bucket.
2. Newly uploaded objects may initially use S3 Standard.
3. An S3 Lifecycle rule evaluates eligible objects.
4. Once the configured age or other rule conditions are met, objects transition to the selected Glacier storage class.
5. If an archived object in Flexible Retrieval or Deep Archive is needed, a restore request is initiated.
6. After restoration completes, the object can be downloaded during the configured restoration period.

Instant Retrieval is different because its objects can be read directly without an archival restore operation.

